s not have\\nthe same symmetries as Kn.\\nIt would be interesting to consider whether the bunkbed conjecture holds after restricting to various\\nclasses of graphs, with a view towards ascertaining whether or not the bunkbed conjecture failing for site\\npercolation is in general the exception or the rule. Indeed, one may ask the following (perhaps optimistic)\\nquestion.\\nQuestion 6.6.5. What is the probability that the assertion in the bunkbed conjecture for site percolation\\nBBC(S1(G)) holds for G ∼G(n, p) the Erd˝os-R´enyi random graph for n large? To what extent does\\nthis probability depend on the behaviour of p as a function of n?\\nThere are of course many other classes of graph one could consider; graphs of diameter 2 (or simply\\nof radius 1), and highly symmetric graphs (e.g. those with transitive automorphism group) would seem\\nto be natural candidates for investigation.\\nReturning now to the original bunkbed conjecture BBC(E(G)), as was discussed in Section 1.5.1, the\\nwork presented here has since been used by Gladkov, Pak and Zimin [63] to construct a counterexample\\nto the bunkbed conjecture. This has constituted a rapid reversal of the direction of the previous literature,\\nwhich had been almost entirely dedicated to attempts to prove that the bunkbed conjecture true.\\nNow that the conjecture is known to be false in general, it is not immediately clear what the next\\ndirection for work in the area should be. One question to ask is for which graphs the bunkbed conjecture--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nThe bunkbed conjecture is not robust to generalisation (https://api.repository.cam.ac.uk/server/api/core/bitstreams/3a9595ae-7514-4075-b8e4-721c65d1cee0/content)\\nciteturn0search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; Thus it remains to construct directed multigraph *D₇*, and remove the conditioning of model *D₆(D₆,T₆,F₆)* on *T₆* and *F₆*, and the assumption that edges are present in only one bunk.\\nL. Hollom                                                                 European Journal of Combinatorics 128 (2025) 104188\\n\\n<visual_element id=\\\"e1\\\">\\nLeft figure: a grey triangle hyperedge labeled *f* with vertices *u* at the top, *v* at the lower left, and *w* at the lower right.\\nRight figure: the corresponding directed graph with vertices *u* (top), *x_f* (left middle), *y_f* (right middle), *v* (bottom left), and *w* (bottom right), connected by multiple directed edges/arrows between the vertices.\\n</visual_element>\\n\\nFig. 5. How a hyperedge *f* in *H₄* is replaced by two vertices and several directed edges in *D₆*. All new edges except for *x_f y_f* are double edges, and are conditioned to appear in both the upper and lower bunks.\\n\\n### 5.2. Directed graphs\\n\\nFinally, we consider directed graphs. We produce the digraph *D₆* from hypergraph *H₄*, and then construct from this the digraph *D₇*.\\n\\nWhen building *D₆* we condition not only on some set of vertices *T₆* which will be posts, but on some set *F₆ ⊆ E(D₆)* of edges which appear in both the upper and lower bunks, with all edges not in *F₆* appearing in precisely one of the two bunks. In particular, *BBC(D₆(D₆,T₆,F₆))* is false.\\n\\nThus it remains to construct directed multigraph *D₇*, and remove the conditioning of model *D₆(D₆,T₆,F₆)* on *T₆* and *F₆*, and the assumption that edges are present in only one bunk. This argument follows very similar lines to those in the previous sections.\\n\\nConstruct *D₇* by attaching to each post vertex *u_i ∈ T₆* *k* vertices *w_{i,1}, . . . , w_{i,k}* (for *k* to be determined later), along with directed edges *u_i w_{i,j}* and *w_{i,j} u_i* for each *i* and *j*. Replace each double edge *xy* in *F₆* with a set of *k* parallel directed edges *e_{x,y,i}* from *x* to *y* for *i ∈ [k]*. Let *A* be the event that all of the following occur:\\n\\n- For each *u_i ∈ T₆*, either the bidirectional vertical edge *u_i^(0) u_i^(1)* is present, or for some *j ∈ [k]*, all four of the directed edges *u_i^(0) w_{i,j}^(0), w_{i,j}^(0) u_i^(0), u_i^(1) w_{i,j}^(1),* and *w_{i,j}^(1) u_i^(1)*, and bidirectional vertical edge *w_{i,j}^(0) w_{i,j}^(1)* are present. In either case, *u_i* is a quasi-post.\\n- For each *xy ∈ F₆*, there is some *i ∈ [k]* such that edge *e_{x,y,i}^{(0)}* is present, and some *j ∈ [k]* such that edge *e_{x,y,j}^{(1)}* is present.\\n\\nLet *B* be the event that any vertex in *V(D₆) \\\\ T₆* is a post, or that any edge in *E(D₆) \\\\ F₆* is present in both the upper and lower bunks. Note that *A ∩ B* is a *(u₁, u₁₀)*-wall event, and note further that *D₇(D)* conditioned on *A* and *B^c* is equivalent to *D₆(D₆,T₆,F₆)*. Thus, as in previous cases,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb{P}\\\\!\\\\left(u_a^{(i)} \\\\xrightarrow{D_7(D_7)} u_b^{(j)} \\\\mid A, B^c\\\\right)\\n=\\n\\\\mathbb{P}\\\\!\\\\left(u_a^{(i)} \\\\xrightarrow{D_6(D_6,T_6,F_6)} u_b^{(j)}\\\\right)\\n=\\n2^{-6}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFollowing identical arguments to those before, it again suffices to prove that inequality (3.1) holds, and all it is left to do is compute \\\\(\\\\mathbb{P}(A^c)\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\mathbb{P}(B^c)\\\\).\\n\\n13--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nCombinatorica (2024) 44:675–690 (https://discovery.ucl.ac.uk/10197006/1/Illingworth_s00493-024-00090-7.pdf)\\nciteturn0search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; Combinatorica (2024) 44:675–690 ... This ﬁnal ingredient, Theorem 3.1, is the only part of the proof where we need our\\ntournament to be transitive: our arguments will show that if t(2, k) = 2k−1 for all k,\\n684\\nCombinatorica (2024) 44:675–690\\nCombining, we obtain\\nt(l, k) ⩽t(l, k −1) + g(l, k,r −1) + 2klr · t(l, k −r)\\n⩽\\n\\u00041\\n\\u0005\\nDεk(log k)1+ε · lk−1\\n2 + 1\\n4 + 1\\n4\\n= Dεk(log k)1+ε · lk−1.\\n⊓⊔\\n3 Upper Bounds for Transitive Tournaments\\nIn this section we prove Theorem 1.3, that is, we show that f (l, k) = lk−1 if l is\\nsufﬁciently large in terms of k. We will follow a similar strategy to that used by\\nLefmann, Rödl, and Thomas [7] when they showed this equality for l ≥(3k)2k, but\\nour bounds and arguments from the previous section will allow us to improve the\\ndependence on k.\\nThe idea of the proof is to ﬁnd a strongly (k −2)-robust vertex, that is, a vertex v\\nsuch that there exists a set C of k −1 colours which contains all colours incident to v\\nand which has the property that for all a ∈C, there is a (k −2)-rainbow with colour\\nset C \\\\ {a} that ends at v, and also one that starts at v. The existence of such a vertex\\nwill allow us to show that every 2-ﬂash in the entire tournament takes its colour from\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nTransitive tournaments under the unconditioned digraph model (E7) (https://www.emergentmind.com/open-problems/transitive-tournament-e7-bunkbed-conjecture)\\nciteturn0search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; Prove that the bunkbed inequality holds for every transitive tournament T_n under the unconditioned directed multigraph model E7, in which two copies of T_n are taken, bidirectional vertical edges are added between corresponding vertices, and a uniformly random subset of all directed edges is retained; that is, establish that BBC(E7, T_n) holds for all n.\\n\\n# Transitive tournaments under the unconditioned digraph model (E7)\\n\\nProve that the bunkbed inequality holds for every transitive tournament T_n under the unconditioned directed multigraph model E7, in which two copies of T_n are taken, bidirectional vertical edges are added between corresponding vertices, and a uniformly random subset of all directed edges is retained; that is, establish that BBC(E7, T_n) holds for all n.\\n\\n### Background\\n\\nAfter showing several generalisations of the bunkbed conjecture to be false, the author isolates a structured family of digraphs—transitive tournaments—as a potentially tractable case in the multigraph setting E7.\\n\\nThis conjecture seeks a positive result in a highly ordered directed setting, contrasting with the negative examples built from graphs with numerous cycles.\\n\\n### References\\n\\nHowever, one case that does seem approachable, though was found by the author to be more difficult than expected, is expressed by the following conjecture. Conjecture BBC{E_7,T_n} holds for T_n, the transitive tournament on n vertices.\\n\\n— The bunkbed conjecture is not robust to generalisation  (2406.01790 - Hollom, 2024) in Section 6 (Conclusion)\\n\\n### Related Problems\\n\\n1.\\n\\nSimple digraph counterexample for the fully directed bunkbed model (E8)  Same Paper\\n\\n2.\\n\\nAcyclic digraphs and the directed bunkbed conjecture  Same Paper\\n\\n3.\\n\\nBunkbed conjecture (original formulation)  2410.02545\\n\\n4.\\n\\nCharacterization of Turánable oriented graphs via D_r  2412.18336\\n\\n5.\\n\\nConjecture: Coarsening monotonicity of E′k  2512.21992\\n\\n6.\\n\\nAll edges in the chain graph are strong  2410.05520\\n\\n7.\\n\\nInstance-optimality on simple graphs (no parallel edges or self-loops)  2410.14638\\n\\n8.\\n\\nDistance-transitive digraphs are Seymour-tight  2603.29626\\n\\n9.\\n\\nLinear out-degree bound for transitive tournament immersion in digraphs  2401.10865\\n\\n10.\\n\\nApproximate form equivalent to Seymour’s conjecture  2603.29626\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nMicrosoft Word - cd1123RRRR.doc (https://cowles.yale.edu/sites/default/files/2022-08/d1123-r4.pdf)\\nciteturn0search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 16.7 years ago; Crawled: 16.7 years ago; *JEL Classification:* D7, D70, D71 ... A constitution is a function which associates with every **N**-tuple (or profile) of transitive preferences a transitive preference called the social preference. ... After I presented this paper at MIT, a graduate student there named Luis Ubeda–Rives told me he had worked out the same neutrality argument as I give in my third proof while he was in Spain nine years ago.\\n\\n*Keywords:* Arrow Impossibility Theorem, pivotal, neutrality\\n\\n*JEL Classification:* D7, D70, D71\\n\\nLet **A** = {A, B, ..., C} be a finite set of at least three alternatives. A transitive preference over **A** is a ranking of the alternatives in **A** from top to bottom, with ties allowed. We consider a society with **N** individuals, each of whom has a (potentially different) transitive preference. A constitution is a function which associates with every **N**-tuple (or profile) of transitive preferences a transitive preference called the social preference.\\n\\n*I wish to thank Ken Arrow, Chris Avery, Don Brown, Ben Polak, Herb Scarf, Chris Shannon, Lin Zhou, and especially Eric Maskin for very helpful comments and advice. I was motivated to think of reproving Arrow’s theorem when I undertook to teach it to George Zettler, a mathematician friend. After I presented this paper at MIT, a graduate student there named Luis Ubeda–Rives told me he had worked out the same neutrality argument as I give in my third proof while he was in Spain nine years ago. He said he was anxious to publish on his own and not jointly, so I encourage the reader to consult his forthcoming working paper. The proofs appearing here appeared in my 1996 CFDP working paper. Proofs 2 and 3 originally used May’s notation, which I have dropped on the advice of Chris Avery.*\\n\\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nCycles in Unions of Transitive Tournaments (https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.WG.2026.3)\\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: last month;   * Part of: Volume: 52nd International Workshop on Graph-Theoretic Concepts in Computer Science (WG 2026) ...   * License:   Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International license ...     A tournament is an orientation of a complete graph. ...  